# TrustVault — .gitignore
#
# Contract: 90-MOC/Gitignore Standard.md. Grouped by what produces the artifact rather than
# by file type, because what you look for later is "what does the build emit", not "where is
# *.png". Every `!` line carries the reason it exists, so the next person tidying up does not
# delete it and discover the effect months later.
#
# The test applied to every line below: if this file vanished, could one command regenerate
# it from the contents of this repo? Yes -> ignore. No -> commit. Unsure -> commit, because
# a bloated repo can be cleaned up at any time and a lost source cannot.
#
# Editor and OS artifacts are deliberately absent. They belong in ~/.config/git/ignore —
# this file is a statement about the project, not about anyone's editor.

# --- Rust build output ---
/target/
/src-tauri/target/

# --- Tauri generated code: capability schemas and permission types ---
/src-tauri/gen/

# --- Node ---
/node_modules/
/dist/

# Cargo.lock and package-lock.json are committed deliberately. A lock file is the only thing
# that makes today's build reproducible next year, so it is source even though a machine
# wrote it.

# --- Vaults: the most important lines in this file ---
# A .tvault that reaches git history is a leaked vault forever. The recovery kit does not
# help, and rewriting history does not help anyone who already cloned.
*.tvault
*.tvault.bak
/vaults/
# Known-answer test vectors are the deliberate exception. They hold throwaway passwords and
# exist precisely to be committed: they are the evidence that a refactor has not silently
# changed the on-disk format (phases/phase-1-vault-core.md, Phase 1 exit gate).
!crates/trustvault-core/tests/vectors/*.tvault

# --- Key material: things that unlock a vault or sign a release ---
*.recovery-kit
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.pem
*.key
.env
.env.*
# The example file is committed so the next person knows which variables a signed release
# build expects, instead of reverse-engineering them from a CI failure. The standard's fixed
# pattern: the real file is ignored, the example file is committed.
!.env.example

# .gitignore is not a security mechanism. It prevents carelessness, not an attacker, and it
# does nothing about a file that is already tracked. A key that has ever entered this repo is
# treated as leaked forever: rotate it first, then deal with the history.

# --- Deliberately NOT ignored, though they look derivative ---
#
# src-tauri/icons/ is generated from src-tauri/icon-source.png by `npm run icons`, so the
# single test says ignore it. It stays committed anyway, for two reasons.
#
# First, it is a shipped artifact: generating it during the build would let a `tauri` CLI
# upgrade change the icon users see with nobody reviewing the diff. Committed, it is pinned.
#
# Second, measured on 2026-08-02: `tauri icon` is not byte-deterministic. Re-running it on a
# byte-identical source produces a different icon.icns every time, so ignoring the directory
# and building it would put unreviewable churn into every CI run rather than removing it.
#
# icon-source.png itself is reproducible — scripts/make-icon.py regenerates it byte-for-byte —
# so the source-to-artifact chain is documented rather than being a binary nobody can recreate.
# The standard's tiebreaker applies: unsure -> commit.
